{"command": "sed -n '131,252p' /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "### User Story 4 - Multi-Tag Invalidation, Resizing, and Lazy Tags (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer invalidates entries matching several tags in one call, choosing\nwhether to remove entries carrying ANY of the supplied tags or only entries\ncarrying ALL of them. The developer can change the cache capacity at runtime:\nshrinking evicts least-recently-used entries while growing never evicts, and a\nfailed resize leaves the cache exactly as it was. Tags may be supplied as\ngenerators (lazy iterables) and are consumed exactly once. Returned values\nstay detached across repeated `get` and `put` calls, including nested lists\nand dicts.\n\n**Why this priority**: Bulk invalidation and runtime resizing extend the core\ntagged-cache value to operational use; they build on Stories 1-3 but are\nindependently testable.\n\n**Independent Test**: Put entries with overlapping tag sets, call\n`invalidate_many` in both modes and assert the removed keys and returned\ncounts; assert empty tag requests remove nothing; assert invalid mode or\ninvalid tags raise `ValueError` with state unchanged. Fill a cache, shrink it\nand assert the LRU victims were evicted with surviving order preserved; grow it\nand assert nothing was evicted; assert a failed `resize` (e.g., `True`, `0`,\n`-1`, `2.5`) leaves capacity and all state unchanged and that the `maxsize`\nproperty reports the current capacity. Pass a generator as `tags` and assert\nit is consumed exactly once and that repeated `get`/`put` never alias the\nstored value.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries with tags `{'a','b'}`, `{'b'}`, and `{'c'}`, **When**\n   `invalidate_many(['a','b'])` (mode `'any'`), **Then** the first two entries\n   are removed, the third survives, and the return value is the number of\n   distinct removed keys (`2`).\n2. **Given** the same entries, **When** `invalidate_many(['a','b'], mode='all')`,\n   **Then** only the entry carrying both `'a'` and `'b'` is removed and the\n   return value is `1`.\n3. **Given** any non-empty cache, **When** `invalidate_many(())` or\n   `invalidate_many([], mode='all')` is called, **Then** zero entries are\n   removed in either mode and state is unchanged (empty requested tags never\n   match; an empty `'all'` request does NOT match every key).\n4. **Given** a cache, **When** `invalidate_many` is called with an invalid\n   `mode` (not `'any'`/`'all'`) or invalid tags (bare string, non-string\n   entry), **Then** `ValueError` is raised BEFORE any mutation and state is\n   unchanged.\n5. **Given** a full cache of `maxsize` entries, **When** `resize(smaller)` is\n   called, **Then** the least-recently-used entries are evicted until the live\n   count equals the new capacity, surviving entries keep their relative LRU\n   order, and the `maxsize` property reports the new capacity.\n6. **Given** a cache, **When** `resize(larger)` is called, **Then** no entry is\n   evicted, the `maxsize` property reports the new capacity, and the cache can\n   then hold up to the new capacity.\n7. **Given** a cache with entries, **When** `resize(True)`, `resize(0)`,\n   `resize(-1)`, or `resize(2.5)` is called, **Then** `ValueError` is raised\n   and capacity, entries, tags, and recency are all unchanged.\n8. **Given** a cache, **When** `put` is called with a generator as `tags`,\n   **Then** the generator is consumed exactly once, the entry is stored with\n   the yielded (deduplicated) tags, and a generator that raises mid-iteration\n   or a bare string is rejected with `ValueError` before any state change.\n9. **Given** a nested value (list/dict), **When** it is `get`-retrieved\n   repeatedly and `put`-replaced repeatedly, **Then** every returned object is\n   a fresh detached deep copy: mutating any returned object or the original\n   input never changes the stored value, and two successive `get`s are not the\n   same object.\n\n### User Story 5 - Time-Limited Entries (TTL) (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer creates a cache with an optional controllable timer (defaulting to\nthe standard monotonic clock) and stores entries with an optional time-to-live.\nEntries without a TTL never expire. Entries with a TTL expire at the absolute\nmoment `timer() + ttl`; a zero TTL is already expired at insertion time, and an\nentry is treated as expired at the exact expiry boundary. Expired entries are\npurged before `get`, `put`, `len`, `resize`, and invalidation, and purging\nnever changes the relative recency of surviving entries. Overwriting an entry\nreplaces its prior expiry. Invalidation counts only live (unexpired) entries.\nA `get` for a missing or expired key raises `KeyError`. Callers who never use\n`timer` or `ttl` observe exactly the previous behavior.\n\n**Why this priority**: Time-limited entries are a core requested capability of\nmilestone 3 and interact with every existing operation (purge-before-operation\nis a cross-cutting correctness rule); they depend on Story 1 storage and\nStory 2 recency/LRU semantics but are independently testable with a fake\ntimer.\n\n**Independent Test**: Build a cache with a fake timer; put an entry with a TTL\nand advance the fake clock to the exact expiry, then assert `get` raises\n`KeyError` and `len` is `0`; put with an invalid TTL (negative, `bool`,\nnon-numeric, non-finite) and assert `ValueError` with state unchanged; put\nwith `ttl=0` and assert the entry is immediately expired; verify a surviving\nentry's recency is unchanged after a purge; verify `ttl=None` entries never\nexpire.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a cache built with a fake timer, **When** `put('a', 1, ttl=5)` is\n   called at `timer()==10`, **Then** the entry is live while `timer() < 15`\n   and `get('a')` raises `KeyError` at `timer() == 15` (exact boundary is\n   expired).\n2. **Given** a cache, **When** `put('a', 1, ttl=0)` is called, **Then** the\n   entry is immediately expired: `get('a')` raises `KeyError` and `len` does\n   not count it.\n3. **Given** an entry with a TTL, **When** the same key is re-put with\n   `ttl=None`, **Then** the entry no longer expires; re-putting with a new\n   finite TTL sets a new expiry from the new `timer()` value (prior expiry\n   replaced).\n4. **Given** one expired and one live entry, **When** `len`, `put`, `get`\n   (of the live key), `resize`, or `invalidate` is called, **Then** the\n   expired entry is removed first, the live entry survives, and the live\n   entry's relative recency is unchanged by the purge.\n5. **Given** an entry carrying tag `'x'` that has expired, **When**\n   `invalidate('x')` is called, **Then** it returns `0` (expired entries are\n   not counted; only live entries are removed and counted).\n6. **Given** `put` with an invalid TTL (negative number, `bool`, non-numeric,\n   or non-finite such as infinity/NaN), **Then** `ValueError` is raised BEFORE\n   any state change (no insert, no replace, no recency change).\n7. **Given** a cache constructed with a custom `timer` callable, **Then** that\n   timer is the sole source of time for all expiry decisions; with the\n   default construction (`timer=None`) the standard monotonic clock is used.\n8. **Given** a cache and `put` calls that never pass `ttl` (and a constructor\n   that never passes `timer`), **Then** behavior is identical to the\n   pre-milestone-3 contract (entries never expire; all R01-R06 behavior\n   unchanged).\n\n### Edge Cases\n"}